Vienna - April ‘26

‘She’s a bitch.’ Hannah grumbled, taking another drag of the slightly stale cigarette she’d pilfered off her PhD student. Some toffy-nosed cow from the Alfred Wegener Institute had won the Julia and Johannes Weertman medal. Hannah knew it should have been hers; the team at BAS had spent the best part of 3 years painstakingly mapping the lateral shear margins of the West Antarctic’s largest ice streams to assess deformation and quantify ice dynamics as its buttressing ice shelves melted into nothingness. All for some society darling, who only the year before had won the Arne Richter Award for EGU Young Scientists, to snatch it from her hand. Even more, Laura and her small research group had the gall to build upon her PhD thesis work on the Fennoscandian Ice Sheet to create a palaeo analogue for Greenland. Hannah had initially been excited to see Dr Müller’s paper in Nature, only for it to call her work pedestrian. Hannah would have rather she’d spat in her face. 

‘Just don’t let her get to you. She's not even a professor, and it's only AWI.’ GP sighed. Hannah had cornered him the day after she’d read Laura’s paper and Schmitz and Lambiase. 2026, had submitted a frankly vicious reply within the week to Nature Communications. They’d been incredibly harsh on Müller et al., challenging her rather shoddy OSL chronology (even mentally-deficient master's students knew the reworking and grain rebleaching potential was far too high for the dates to anchor the time series), and ripping holes into the varve records they'd presented. Hannah had wondered how such glaringly obvious overcounting had been published in such a prestigious journal.​

She’d gotten an email from Laura the same day her response had been published. Some bullshit about getting off on the wrong foot and the offer of collaboration that had promptly been banished to the deleted folder of Hannah’s Outlook. GP had ended up finishing off her lunch, forgotten during a monologue about how entitled Dr Müller had been to think that Hannah would want anything to do with her after dragging her thesis work through the mud so publicly. GP had been a postdoc at The Scot Polar Research Institute when she’d started her PhD. He’d been one of her supervisors, though they’d been more like friends as he’d not really had any true seniority over her. He’d grown to like her when she’d not told a soul he’d left his tephra samples in HCL so long in the fume hood the foil had corroded away. He’d liked her even more when she’d stayed late for the month to help him with the tedious resampling and sieving he had to repeat on 98 metres of marine sediment at 5 cm intervals for the pilot study he’d let dissolve while marking a pile of first-year exams. 

‘I’m sorry, Professor Schmitz?’ 

They turned. Hannah breathed out the acrid cigarette smoke, her arms wrapped around her torso to protect against the spring chill in the air. The woman before her was tall and fine. She had a square jaw and a prominent nose that added character to her made-up face. Long caramel hair was pulled up in an elegant chignon she’d no doubt paid for. For an academic at a public European research institution, Dr Müller seemingly had expensive taste.  Hannah had seen the gown she was wearing on the rails in Selfridges; far too much to splurge on with her meagre salary even as a research lead. Her eyes roamed the structured plum number that hugged Laura’s curves and complemented the creamy complexion of her skin, leaving Hannah feeling decidedly frumpy. She was in a dress she’d bought for the AGU gala before she’d had her first daughter, and though she’d worked hard to get back into it, the fit around the bust and stomach never looked the same. 

‘And you are?’ Hannah said dismissively, flicking the ash from her cigarette. She knew who Laura was, she’d spent evenings pouring over her research output, dissecting her masters and PhD thesis before bed for two weeks to find all to find her flaws. 

‘Laura…Dr Müller - I work at AWI, ’ she smiled nervously, her hand outstretched to Hannah. The ostentatious diamond wedding set on her finger twinkling under the street lighting. Hannah doesn’t take her hand, watching Laura wither under her gaze as she takes another slow drag of her cigarette. 

‘Ok?’ ​

‘I wanted to speak with you - I sent you an email a few months ago, but I think it must have been missed. I know how busy you are; it's totally fine - I really liked your talk, by the way.’  Hannah liked watching her squirm. 

GP put her out of her misery. ‘Thanks,’ he smiled politely. Congratulations on your prize.’ He had to resist laughter as he felt Hannah’s head snap towards him, eyes boring into the side of his skull. 

‘Thank you! That means so much, Professor Lambiase - your work on NEGIS and its tempo-spatial evolution is extraordinary.’ Dr Müller beamed. Hannah rolled her eyes; she had spent the whole day hearing the praise heaped on Laura. Surely she could drop the cloying faux-humility act and ceaseless brown-nosing for a second and say ‘thanks’. 

‘We worked very hard on it,’ GP agreed, his hand resting on Hannah’s shoulder. 

‘Of course - Professor Schmitz… Hannah, I think we –’ 

‘Professor Schmitz is fine.’ Hannah’s voice was curt. 

‘Oh… Ok. Sorry. I um…I really valued your comments on the paper about the FIS,’ she began, though she looked discernibly less confident now. Dr Müller deserved to be cut down to size, Hannah thought, as Laura tried to address the comments she’d made in her response before repeating her offer of collaboration. ‘BAS and AWI already have the shared venture with EPICA,’ Hannah observed, a bead of nervous sweat rolling down her long neck. Victory. 

‘I don’t believe I have time for such projects –’ Hannah began.​

‘Your response was incredibly passionate,’ Laura’s eyes flicked to GP, as if asking for help, or reassurance, or any sort of sign out of the lion’s den she’d tottered into on her designer heels. Suddenly left with the feeling she was a young child in a costume before the real adults. Like how she’d felt when she first started with Toto, her face growing crimson as he’d introduced her to his inner circle. His ex-wife's side of the bed was still warm when he’d moved her into his home. Professor Wolff had been her master's dissertation supervisor just over a decade ago now. Tall and brooding, she’d done shamefully little when following him into his office for a periodical he insisted she read, but instead kissed her the second the door closed. It was certain, she remembered. Unlike the sweet but stupid boyfriend she’d lived with at the time. His broad body against hers as he whispered his guilt at being unable to resist her until she'd graduated. Toto had poured honey into her ear about her astute thoughts, her developed sense of humour, or the maturity she had when tackling interdepartmental research projects. 

Toto knew the words to capture her; he understood her mind, and challenged her to move forward. He was evolved and elevated, not to mention married to another woman when he’d made a reservation at the Koenigshof to fuck her in. He’d been younger then; still with the stamina and virility a 20-something-year-old mistress wanted. Laura was aware she was far older than the now Professor Stoddart, formerly Dr Wolff, had been when Toto had given her divorce papers. She’d seen it as his gaze had lingered one evening on the non-existent arse of her PhD student. Laura had struggled to look Courtney in the eye for weeks after. Would Courtney do to her what she had done to Susie? The expensive colourist who blended away her growing collection of silver hairs every 6 weeks. Her regimen of dermatologist-provided skin care that she wouldn't even go a night without did little to smooth the growing lines on her forehead. Laura had chickened out of the three Botox appointments she’d made so far, but a fourth attempt was scheduled next weekend. Laura wore her joy on her face; laughter lines that grew more prominent, making her feel haggard. He was older now; she’d tried to rationalise it. Cooing reassurance in his ear about the little blue pills in his nightstand he sometimes needed.

‘My response has nothing to do with my schedule, Dr Müller.’ 

‘I know, I know…It’s just–’ 

‘This isn't a point of discussion. My answer is no.’ Hannah stubbed the glowing orange end of her cigarette out against the old brickwork. If only Hannah could get rid of Laura and the threads of doubt she’d unravelled in her head so easily. ‘GP, I suppose we should regroup before they send out the search party.’ Her words were less a suggestion, but more an order he knew better than to argue with. ​

‘Sounds like we need to get you to the bar.’ GP leaned in, a chuckle escaping his mouth when displeasure spread across Hannah’s usually sweet face. ‘Have a good evening, congrats again on the prize, Dr Mü– Laura.’ he gave her a smile that didn't reach his eyes as the pair from BAS headed back inside the banquet hall. Laura stood alone for a few moments. The smell of cigarette smoke lingered on the breeze. This should have been one of the proudest moments of her life, and yet she felt hollow. Expensive mauve lipstick smeared as she chewed at her lip. If she cried, it would be over; if Hannah knew she had gotten to her, she would win. Laura felt utterly pathetic; academic careers were sustained by debate in the community. Her own work was routinely pulled apart, even by her own husband at dinner. There was no need for Hannah to be so defensive.

​

The gala dinner was how they usually went; lukewarm food, bottles of shit wine littering the table. There wasn't even real glassware; just the expensive plastic ones with no real weight to them. Laura could have laughed; all the research about microplastics that had come out, including a few dry talks she’d sat through earlier in the afternoon, and the EGU catering couldn't even afford reusable. It stopped bothering her after the third generous pour of red wine. Her hand stopped shaking after the fourth. Toto lost in quiet conversation with some fluvial geomorphologist he wanted to poach to his department; he’d originally said he wouldn't be able to watch her accept her medal - some departmental thing he couldn't avoid. Besides, he'd seen her win enough awards he’d said she didn't need him to hold her hand through it anymore. But to her delight, he’d come. Toto had watched the other talk running when Laura had presented her research, and ignored her over the buffet lunch. He’d called her immature when she’d asked him about it in the hotel, leaving her trying to fix the mascara spreading down her cheeks when he’d gone to the hotel bar; insisting she must calm down and stop acting so childishly. 
​
It all changed when his large hand slid around her waist at cocktail hour, when she’d been lost in fiery debate with James. Professor Allison was the deputy chair of the Department of Earth Sciences at Bremen. Some wizard of atmospheric numerical modelling who enjoyed whipping out the Navier-Stokes equation at every possible opportunity. His research bored Laura to tears, but he was fun to talk to about other things. James swore far too much, and took himself far less seriously than Toto did. She felt infantile when Toto joined them, as his hand slid to her hip. The conversation about Föhn winds spluttered out to nothing as Toto manoeuvred them back to her. How proud he was of his distinguished little wife. The hope that things had settled between them disintegrated instantaneously. Though maybe this was what settled was like between them, she was just another prize that sat in his home like all the other medals or awards for his research. Laura was months away from 36. She’d been made head of her working group at the beginning of the year, only the step down from the highest seniority there was at AWI. Her smile had wavered, just for a moment; hot shame in her chest as she tried to swallow down the rising doubt. She'd once loved how Toto had treated her; he wasn't overly affectionate or emotional. Laura knew where she stood with him and liked how he saw her. As if she was something more than a young master's student. In all his wisdom, he'd seen her as special, that her work had some unique edge that made her better. The older she got, the more it started to feel like he had liked the curve of her arse and the naive lust in her eyes as she’d watch him lecture. 

It had, in the end, probably been for the best that the glasses the gala had provided were plastic; especially after Laura had thrown the second one towards Hannah. One small comment about drumlin genesis muttered under Hannah’s breath had lit the tinder between them. Laura felt slightly guilty as she slumped into the back of their Uber; Toto had dragged her away, muttering that she was embarrassing him as she cried out how Hannah was old - how no one under the age of 40 paid any attention to anything other than the deforming bed model. And that perhaps it would do Hannah some good to get out from her beloved numerical modelling and do some real work - especially as Laura had seen exactly how shoddy her geomorphic interpretations of the FIS had been in Hannah’s PhD thesis. 

She’d gotten especially cross when Hannah had patronisingly brought up the erosional landscapes of Iceland or Sweden, and the examples of undisturbed stratigraphies in some drumlin cores found there. Besides, it was only one small part of the puzzle. Hannah had frowned condescendingly, her voice soft as if gently correcting a struggling first-year student. She suggested Laura needed to mature her critical thinking skills before she attended the next conference if she was unable to handle the meagre concept of equifinality in lighthearted after-dinner discussions. Laura had been willing to walk away until she’d heard laughter; it had cut through her like a knife. She had the medal, the paper; everything she knew Hannah had wanted, and yet she felt 12 years old again. Excluded and belittled. Laura had regretted it the second the almost empty plastic wineglass left her fingertips. It hardly felt like the cinematic crescendo to a fight, the plastic glass missing Hannah’s head and bouncing on the marbled floor. The second she’d thrown had hit her, though thankfully just leaving a stain on the ugly, unflattering gown she’d been wearing rather than an injury. Hannah had remained calm; it made Laura even more furious, as if her feelings were pathetic, like she wasn’t even worth getting cross with. She wanted that bitch to fight back- so passionate in her response paper and utterly tepid in person. Instead, she was the only one slurring in an evening gown, hair a mess as she was dragged away by a man old enough to be her father. 

Laura felt Toto’s disgust with her simmering as the blurring lights of Vienna twinkled in the window of the car. The drive back to the hotel was silent; as was the lift, and the walk to the small suite they were staying in. He’d insisted she use his card and get somewhere proper to stay outside of the budget AWI would give her for accommodation. Especially as Vienna was his home town. 

‘Can you help me with my zip?’ She murmured, watching as he hung up his suit jacket and removed his cufflinks. 

‘Are you so drunk you can’t even work a zip?’ His voice was curt. 

‘No—’ 

‘Just so drunk that you start screaming like a child at one of your peers—’ 

‘She started it—’ 

‘So you throw a glass at her? Really?’ 

‘Did you even read her response to us?’ 

‘Well, perhaps you shouldn’t start arguments you aren’t clever enough to finish then.’ 

‘I won the fucking medal - not her!’ Laura’s voice rose as she did, still slightly unsteady on the heels she currently lacked the dexterity to unbuckle from around her ankle.

‘And yet you wither under the first bit of real criticism. I thought I made you better than that.’  His large hand turned her away from him; Toto found the small zipper on the back of her dress. 

‘It was a lapse—’ She felt herself submit. Toto was already a Professor before they’d even met, she’d once felt honoured to be guided by his all-knowing hand. Now it felt suffocating. Laura wanted a lover, someone tender she could let herself be gentle with. Instead, every conversation had started to feel like an oral defence. 

‘It will not happen again.’ Toto was firm as he pushed the dress from her shoulders. 

‘I know.’  

‘You are at AWI because of me. Do you understand?’ 

‘That’s not fair,’ she rebuffed. She stepped out of her dress, her hand reaching for the plush hotel robe to pull around her exposed body. ‘You introduced me to Andrew—’

‘You got that job because Professor Shovlin and I used to work together, and he owed me a favour.’ 

‘I still had to interview—’ 

‘Formality.’ He walked towards her; his immense hand came to rest gently on her cheek. It was almost tender. ‘Do you really think I could let my wife languish at some second-rate Dutch university?’ 

Laura had initially gotten a Post-Doc offer from Utrecht; she’d liked IMAU and the research group there; the only issue was it would have meant spending a few nights a week in the city away from their home in Bremen. 

‘What you do reflects on me. Understand?’ 

Laura felt so small as she gazed up at Toto, the man she loved, as he so casually cut her back down to size. For years she’d dodged accusations of him being the reason for her success, worked herself into near burnout for years to make those claims seem false. Now she had infallible proof they were all true. 

‘I understand.’ 

